[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performance Overhead and Memory Leaks Caused by Improper Pattern Usage"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Design patterns provide proven solutions to recurring architectural problems. However, applying these patterns blindly or failing to understand their resource implications can lead to severe performance bottlenecks, unnecessary CPU overhead, and insidious memory leaks."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Singleton Pattern and Global State Leaks"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Singleton pattern restricts instantiation of a class to a single object. While useful for managing shared resources like database connections, it frequently introduces memory leaks in garbage-collected languages because it maintains a global reference to objects, preventing them from being collected."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"class LeakySingleton {
    private static instance: LeakySingleton;
    private cache: Array<any> = [];

    private constructor() {}

    public static getInstance(): LeakySingleton {
        if (!LeakySingleton.instance) {
            LeakySingleton.instance = new LeakySingleton();
        }
        return LeakySingleton.instance;
    }

    public addData(data: any): void {
        this.cache.push(data); // Unbounded growth leads to memory leaks
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Observer Pattern and Detached Event Listeners"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Observer pattern is essential for decoupled architectures, such as event-driven systems. However, if a subject holds strong references to its observers without proper unsubscription mechanisms, the observed objects will remain in memory indefinitely, even if the observer is no longer visually or functionally active in the application."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always implement explicit unsubscribe methods or utilize WeakMap and WeakSet structures when managing dynamic listener registrations."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Ensure UI components detach their event listeners during their destruction or unmounting lifecycle phases."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Mitigation Strategies and Best Practices"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"To prevent performance degradation, developers must profile memory usage continuously and apply architectural safeguards."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"// Using WeakRef or WeakMap to prevent retaining unused objects
class SafeObserverRegistry {
    private listeners = new WeakMap<object, Function>();

    public register(target: object, callback: Function): void {
        this.listeners.set(target, callback);
    }

    public getCallback(target: object): Function | undefined {
        return this.listeners.get(target);
    }
}"},{"order":13,"paragraph":"By being mindful of reference lifecycles, understanding garbage collection mechanisms, and avoiding the overuse of global states, developers can harness the full power of design patterns without compromising application performance."}]
